    "copy-from": "hflesh",
    "fuel_data": {
      "energy": "9610 kJ",
      "explosion_data": {
        "chance_hot": 2000,
        "chance_cold": 4000,
        "factor": 0.05,
        "fiery": false,
        "size_factor": 0.2
      }
    },
    "//": "Taking 70 Kg, 18% fat content, 16% protein, 1% carbs for an average human body, then energy densities of 37.6 kJ/g for fat and 16.7 kJ/g for protein and carbs, there are 12600 g fat + 11200 g protein + 700 g carbs = 473760 kJ + 187040 kJ + 11690 kJ respectively = 672490 kJ = 672.49 MJ, then assuming a density of 1 kg/m^3 for the human body = 672.49 MJ / 70 L = ~9.61 MJ/L",
    "//2": "This is ignoring the water content because I'm not dealing with temperatures needed for phase changes + heat of vaporization + steam production",
    "//3": "According to https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12051324/ 'human tissue' has an 'effective heat of combustion' of 17 kJ/g, which equals 17 MJ/L assuming the previous density. This paper was followed by at least two others, the problem is I can't access the paper beyond the abstract"
  },
